<title>CANADA: Cargill's Alberta beef packers end strike.</title>
<headline>Cargill's Alberta beef packers end strike.</headline>
<dateline>WINNIPEG 1997-08-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Cargill Ltd's striking Alberta beef packers voted 53 percent in favour of the company's contract offer over the long weekend and the High River plant resumed operation Tuesday morning, a Cargill official said.</p>
<p>&quot;It'll be a couple of weeks to get back to 4,000 head of cattle a day,&quot; Cargill vice president Barb Isman told Reuters.</p>
<p>Some 861 of the 1,600 United Food and Commercial Workers union members voted 53 percent to accept a C$1.50 an hour raise over three and a half years, Isman said.</p>
<p>Workers had previously rejected Cargill's offer of a graduated C$1.50 an hour raise over four years.</p>
<p>The UFCW sought C$1.95 over three years.</p>
<p>The plant, one of Canada's largest, usually slaughters 4,000 head of cattle a day.</p>
<p>((Gilbert Le Gras, Winnipeg Reuters bureau 204 947 3548))</p>
